{"name":"Lichen Blue","hex":"#5d89b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lichen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many symbiotic organisms, being associations of algae and fungi, often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.","Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lichen"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lichen","blocks":[{"text":"A lichen is a hybrid colony of algae or cyanobacteria living symbiotically among filaments of multiple fungus species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or \"skin\", in a mutualistic relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis into biological context."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg/330px-Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichen","wikidata":"Q43142"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/1280px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_(7).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/500px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/1280px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/500px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaloplaca_marina.JPG"},{"srcset":"h